 - *C2_PARAMKEY_BITRATE_MODE*: This parameter can be used to switch between
@@ -314,7 +321,31 @@ bitrate. This mode is preferable for video conferencing where maintaining a
 stable bitrate is more important than quality. When using VBR the encoder will
 be allowed to dynamically adjust the bitrate to maintain a constant quality. As
 the mediacodec framework does not provide facilities to configure the peak
-bitrate when using VBR, it is currently always set to 2x the target bitrate.
+bitrate when using VBR, it is currently always set to 2x the target bitrate, or
+to the most the level allows if that is less.
+
+The following parameters exist only if the encoder device has the controls for
+them, and are static:
+
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_BITRATE_MODE* offers the modes of the device's
+*V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_BITRATE_MODE* menu. The constant quality mode
+(*C2Config::BITRATE_IGNORE*) takes its quality from *C2_PARAMKEY_QUALITY*, which
+can change at run-time.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_BITRATE*, *C2_PARAMKEY_PICTURE_QUANTIZATION*, *C2_PARAMKEY_GOP*
+(B-frames) and *C2_PARAMKEY_TEMPORAL_LAYERING* are limited to what the device's
+controls allow. With B-frames the encoder returns its output in stream order and
+*C2_PARAMKEY_PIPELINE_DELAY* is one frame longer than *C2_PARAMKEY_INPUT_DELAY*,
+so a client that waits for an output sooner stalls the stream.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_COLOR_ASPECTS* sets the colorimetry of the raw frames, including
+the transfer function, and *C2_PARAMKEY_VUI_COLOR_ASPECTS* reports what the
+stream carries.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_HDR_STATIC_INFO* and the P010 entry of
+*C2_PARAMKEY_PIXEL_FORMAT* exist only for HEVC Main 10, which needs a device
+that takes P010 frames.
+- The *C2_PARAMKEY_PROFILE_LEVEL* of the output reports the level the device
+chose when it differs from the ceiling the client asked for.
+- The picture size and the bitrate start at the device's lowest value, and not
+below 64 samples on a side and 20000 bit/s.
 
 The following parameters are dynamic, and can be freely adjusted at run-time:
 
@@ -326,16 +357,41 @@ framerate.
 - *C2_PARAMKEY_REQUEST_SYNC_FRAME*: This parameter can be used to request
 additional key frames in addition to the periodic ones requested through the
 *C2_PARAMKEY_SYNC_FRAME_INTERVAL* parameter.
+- *C2_PARAMKEY_OPERATING_RATE* and *C2_PARAMKEY_PRIORITY* change the system
+resources the instance needs, see below.
+
+### System Resources:
+
+The component store reports the capacity of the device, and each encoder the
+resources it needs, so that Android can tell what is left
+(*MediaCodec.getGloballyAvailableResources()*), under two vendor ids:
+
+- Codec sessions (*C2PlatformConfig::VENDOR_START*): the sum of the concurrent
+instance bounds of the codecs the store offers; every instance needs one.
+- Pixel rate (*VENDOR_START* + 1), against *ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.video_pixel_rate*:
+an instance needs the width times the height times the frame rate (the
+operating rate, if it is set) of its stream when it runs in real time, and
+nothing when it does not. Omit the property to report sessions only.
+
+A start that would take the pixel rate of all instances past the capacity fails
+with *C2_NO_MEMORY*, which Android raises as *ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCE*; the
+first instance is always admitted. A product states as the concurrent instance
+bound the number of the largest streams its memory holds, not the number of
+streams the host serves.
 
 ### Supported Input Pixel Formats:
 
-The V4L2 encoder supports various input pixel formats, however frames are
-currently always passed to the V4L2 encoder in the NV12 format. If a video frame
-using a different pixel format is passed to the encoder, format conversion will
-be performed to convert the frame to the NV12 format.
+Frames are passed to the V4L2 encoder in the NV12 format, or in P010 for HEVC
+Main 10. A frame in a different pixel format is converted to that format, except
+packed RGB frames (RGBA_8888 and RGBX_8888 blocks), which the device takes as
+they are; 10-bit RGB for an 8-bit encoder is not converted.
 
 ### Additional Features:
 
+A device that holds the bitrate by leaving frames out returns the output buffer
+of such a frame with the error flag and no data. The work item of the frame
+finishes without an output, and the items behind it are not held up.
+
 To improve the resilience of H.264 video streams when data is missing, SPS and
